00:16:15.886 --> 00:16:18.312
Yeah, I wish it was under better circumstances,

418
00:16:18.312 --> 00:16:19.805
but I feel like the conversation

419
00:16:19.805 --> 00:16:23.459
we're about to have is an important one.

420
00:16:23.459 --> 00:16:25.085
To help us all better understand

421
00:16:25.085 --> 00:16:28.118
what's happening in Afghanistan, how we got there,

422
00:16:28.118 --> 00:16:32.234
and perhaps some solutions for all of us

423
00:16:32.234 --> 00:16:35.223
to avail ourselves to participate

424
00:16:35.223 --> 00:16:37.953
in ameliorating the hardship that's going on there.

425
00:16:37.953 --> 00:16:39.955
But before we even get into any of that,

426
00:16:39.955 --> 00:16:42.954
I think it would be instructive for each of you

427
00:16:42.954 --> 00:16:46.332
to recap your relationship with Afghanistan,

428
00:16:46.332 --> 00:16:49.126
and your experience there.

429
00:16:49.126 --> 00:16:50.084
Sure.

430
00:16:50.084 --> 00:16:52.501
So I was actually born there.

431
00:16:54.048 --> 00:16:58.424
My parents and my grandparents are from Afghanistan,

432
00:16:58.424 --> 00:16:59.546
were from Afghanistan.

433
00:16:59.546 --> 00:17:01.745
And when I was a medical student,

434
00:17:01.745 --> 00:17:04.652
I had an opportunity to go back and help out.

435
00:17:04.652 --> 00:17:06.936
I think all of us grew up

436
00:17:06.936 --> 00:17:10.630
with this idea to give back to the community.

437
00:17:10.630 --> 00:17:12.122
That was something that was instilled in us

438
00:17:12.122 --> 00:17:15.194
by our grandparents and our parents.

439
00:17:15.194 --> 00:17:19.194
And back in 2002, I went back there to help out.

440
00:17:20.449 --> 00:17:23.117
I was with Doctors Without Borders,

441
00:17:23.117 --> 00:17:26.077
and with some other phenomenal individuals.

442
00:17:26.077 --> 00:17:29.143
This was the post Hollomon era.

443
00:17:29.143 --> 00:17:32.875
And I was very passionate about helping women and children,

444
00:17:32.875 --> 00:17:34.708
as was everybody else.

445
00:17:35.938 --> 00:17:39.305
And it was one of the most beautiful times of my life,

446
00:17:39.305 --> 00:17:43.280
because there was a rebirth of hope, where

447
00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:48.103
after a very prolonged period of darkness and hopelessness,

448
00:17:48.103 --> 00:17:50.928
suddenly an opportunity became available

449
00:17:50.928 --> 00:17:54.775
for men and women, but especially for women

450
00:17:54.775 --> 00:17:59.040
to find freedom, to find their voice, to educate themselves,

451
00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:00.288
to interact with each other,

452
00:18:00.288 --> 00:18:03.475
and to be really accepted as human beings,

453
00:18:03.475 --> 00:18:06.247
in a society which shrouded them,

454
00:18:06.247 --> 00:18:10.354
which just deleted them from the face of this earth.

455
00:18:10.354 --> 00:18:12.736
And so to have been in that environment,

456
00:18:12.736 --> 00:18:15.819
where you saw this growth, this hope,

457
00:18:16.915 --> 00:18:20.118
and the continuation of that hope over many, many years,

458
00:18:20.118 --> 00:18:23.515
and now to see this devastating effect

459
00:18:23.515 --> 00:18:27.700
of taking away that hope from them overnight,

460
00:18:27.700 --> 00:18:29.450
is just catastrophic.

461
00:18:30.329 --> 00:18:33.036
So even though Dean and I

462
00:18:33.036 --> 00:18:35.083
are in the field of neurology and brain health,

463
00:18:35.083 --> 00:18:36.760
and we talk about everything brain health,

464
00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:39.226
I think it's very, very important for us

465
00:18:39.226 --> 00:18:42.835
to talk about something that is powerful,

466
00:18:42.835 --> 00:18:45.250
that not just is a calamity

467
00:18:45.250 --> 00:18:48.634
that has affected a particular population,

468
00:18:48.634 --> 00:18:51.486
I think it affects all of us as human beings,

469
00:18:51.486 --> 00:18:55.068
because it's an overnight extinction

470
00:18:55.068 --> 00:18:58.901
of 16 million plus human beings on this earth,

471
00:18:59.807 --> 00:19:03.127
these women who were given hope, and it's worse than that.

472
00:19:03.127 --> 00:19:05.315
You're taking away that hope from them.

473
00:19:05.315 --> 00:19:06.148
Yeah.

474
00:19:06.148 --> 00:19:08.865
Dean, I wanna hear your experience as well,

475
00:19:08.865 --> 00:19:11.220
but Ayesha, this was...

476
00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:14.326
So when you returned to Afghanistan, it was around 2003?

477
00:19:14.326 --> 00:19:15.159
Yes.

478
00:19:15.159 --> 00:19:16.623
So we're talking post 9/11,

479
00:19:16.623 --> 00:19:20.596
it was that era of optimism and a sense of possibility,

480
00:19:20.596 --> 00:19:22.334
where rights were being restored,

481
00:19:22.334 --> 00:19:24.921
and everything seemed to be moving

482
00:19:24.921 --> 00:19:27.940
in an upward positive trajectory,

483
00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:29.835
at least with respect to human rights.

484
00:19:29.835 --> 00:19:31.941
Yes. Absolutely.

485
00:19:31.941 --> 00:19:36.907
When people watch the news or watch clips from Afghanistan,

486
00:19:36.907 --> 00:19:40.767
and how the west and the United States went there

487
00:19:40.767 --> 00:19:44.035
to help rebuild the country and provide security,

488
00:19:44.035 --> 00:19:47.575
they don't realize that the biggest asset

489
00:19:47.575 --> 00:19:50.742
was providing opportunities for women,

490
00:19:53.543 --> 00:19:56.724
for this population that never had that opportunity.

491
00:19:56.724 --> 00:19:58.046
And for them to become

492
00:19:58.046 --> 00:20:02.762
very successful models of progress in a society.

493
00:20:02.762 --> 00:20:04.961
I mean, just imagine half of the society

494
00:20:04.961 --> 00:20:08.680
not being involved in making decisions for the country,

495
00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:11.915
suddenly all of them came into forefront.

496
00:20:11.915 --> 00:20:13.998
And it was this beautiful

497
00:20:15.215 --> 00:20:18.856
regeneration of a culture and a society,

498
00:20:18.856 --> 00:20:23.771
where music was brought back, where art came back.

499
00:20:23.771 --> 00:20:28.354
And when you provide that security, creativity is born.

500
00:20:29.904 --> 00:20:34.580
And so we saw the manifestation of that security.

501
00:20:34.580 --> 00:20:36.467
The very thing that makes me so sad

502
00:20:36.467 --> 00:20:38.224
is that it's just not there anymore.

503
00:20:38.224 --> 00:20:39.057
Yeah.

504
00:20:39.057 --> 00:20:40.015
Can I ask?

505
00:20:40.015 --> 00:20:44.045
Just to set the context for listeners who might not know,

506
00:20:44.045 --> 00:20:47.588
when the Taliban came in in the mid to late nineties

507
00:20:47.588 --> 00:20:51.164
and ruled there, music was like...

508
00:20:51.164 --> 00:20:52.462
You couldn't play recorded music,

509
00:20:52.462 --> 00:20:55.264
photographs were outlawed,

510
00:20:55.264 --> 00:20:58.593
certain books were outlawed, film entertainment,

511
00:20:58.593 --> 00:21:00.684
that was outlawed by punishment of death.

512
00:21:00.684 --> 00:21:01.517
Right?

513
00:21:01.517 --> 00:21:02.746
Is that correct?
Like recording tape

514
00:21:02.746 --> 00:21:05.109
would be hung from the trees as a warning.

515
00:21:05.109 --> 00:21:05.942
Yeah.

516
00:21:05.942 --> 00:21:09.228
So then you're saying, this openness brings it all back?

517
00:21:09.228 --> 00:21:10.642
Absolutely.

518
00:21:10.642 --> 00:21:11.559
Absolutely.

519
00:21:16.063 --> 00:21:20.396
It's difficult to perceive that limited way of life,

520
00:21:21.489 --> 00:21:25.687
where anything that almost brings pleasure

521
00:21:25.687 --> 00:21:27.520
is completely negated.

522
00:21:28.381 --> 00:21:31.548
And so in the absence of that freedom,

523
00:21:37.219 --> 00:21:38.969
it's not just helping

524
00:21:39.967 --> 00:21:43.393
a particular set of population live a normal life,

525
00:21:43.393 --> 00:21:46.277
you're basically killing progress in many ways.

526
00:21:46.277 --> 00:21:50.107
And Dean and I actually saw this metamorphosis

527
00:21:50.107 --> 00:21:53.492
that was happening early in the early 2000s,

528
00:21:53.492 --> 00:21:54.721
when we were working there together.

529
00:21:54.721 --> 00:21:57.005
We actually met there during that time,

530
00:21:57.005 --> 00:22:00.357
and we got married there, we dated there.

531
00:22:00.357 --> 00:22:03.357
One of the most amazing experiences.

532
00:22:04.530 --> 00:22:05.795
And you were talking about

533
00:22:05.795 --> 00:22:09.128
what was during the Taliban in the nineties,

534
00:22:09.128 --> 00:22:10.880
and what happened.

535
00:22:10.880 --> 00:22:14.867
I was there right after the 9/11, end of 2001,

536
00:22:14.867 --> 00:22:18.488
I was at NIH doing these wonky research in building 10,

537
00:22:18.488 --> 00:22:20.123
where you put probes into the basal ganglia,

538
00:22:20.123 --> 00:22:22.232
and all this stuff.

539
00:22:22.232 --> 00:22:23.905
And because of my background in management,

540
00:22:23.905 --> 00:22:25.735
they asked me to go back to Afghanistan

541
00:22:25.735 --> 00:22:27.935
to see if for three months, if you can help out.

542
00:22:27.935 --> 00:22:30.134
And I went back, it was supposed to be three months,

543
00:22:30.134 --> 00:22:31.843
I'm not gonna give up that career.

544
00:22:31.843 --> 00:22:33.920
And the first day I go there...

545
00:22:33.920 --> 00:22:34.772
And by the way,

546
00:22:34.772 --> 00:22:38.683
at the time I'm living on a lake in Reston, Virginia.

547
00:22:38.683 --> 00:22:43.322
And I land into the airport, this is at the beginning.

548
00:22:43.322 --> 00:22:45.065
There's no light, there are no lights,

549
00:22:45.065 --> 00:22:46.208
there's no electricity.

550
00:22:46.208 --> 00:22:48.200
People are just walking around

551
00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:51.283
almost like a zombie esque, no color.

552
00:22:52.172 --> 00:22:55.538
The first thing I do is drive through the city,

553
00:22:55.538 --> 00:22:58.465
and there's no building standing in this one street,

554
00:22:58.465 --> 00:23:00.793
that's famous street, third street.

555
00:23:00.793 --> 00:23:01.773
And no building.

556
00:23:01.773 --> 00:23:03.811
And you see these little heads popping up,

557
00:23:03.811 --> 00:23:05.605
and you look around like, "Oh my gosh, why...

558
00:23:05.605 --> 00:23:08.736
Shouldn't they be told not to play there?"

559
00:23:08.736 --> 00:23:10.940
"Oh, no, that's where they live."

560
00:23:10.940 --> 00:23:12.940
So it was the emotional,

561
00:23:13.981 --> 00:23:16.255
the visceral nature was just so great.

562
00:23:16.255 --> 00:23:18.288
Three months turned into three years,

563
00:23:18.288 --> 00:23:19.467
where the president asked me

564
00:23:19.467 --> 00:23:22.278
to stay and rebuild the healthcare system.

565
00:23:22.278 --> 00:23:23.908
But what I saw was...

566
00:23:23.908 --> 00:23:26.105
And this is critical, because I think this is important

567
00:23:26.105 --> 00:23:27.484
for all of us as human beings.

568
00:23:27.484 --> 00:23:30.407
I think what the most important part of this conversation

569
00:23:30.407 --> 00:23:33.546
that you're bringing, and similar kind of conversation is,

570
00:23:33.546 --> 00:23:37.213
for people to experience the breadth of human expression,

571
00:23:37.213 --> 00:23:41.880
that it is that person named Malia, Alala, or something,

572
00:23:42.918 --> 00:23:45.840
is actually my sister, my daughter, my mother.

573
00:23:45.840 --> 00:23:46.860
It's the same.

574
00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:48.485
They're the same people.

575
00:23:48.485 --> 00:23:52.469
Now in 2002, I went to the restaurants, there was no taste.

576
00:23:52.469 --> 00:23:55.191
Food had no taste, no color,

577
00:23:55.191 --> 00:23:56.940
buildings had no color, nothing.

578
00:23:56.940 --> 00:23:58.367
People would wear just gray.

579
00:23:58.367 --> 00:24:00.571
Because those are luxuries.

580
00:24:00.571 --> 00:24:05.026
But as soon as freedom came, normal as it was,

581
00:24:05.026 --> 00:24:08.972
within two months, you saw color, sound,

582
00:24:08.972 --> 00:24:11.639
music, taste, smell, good smell.

583
00:24:12.644 --> 00:24:14.558
Everything just manifest.

584
00:24:14.558 --> 00:24:17.723
That was such an incredible mental state for me,

585
00:24:17.723 --> 00:24:19.773
to see, "Oh, wow!"

586
00:24:19.773 --> 00:24:21.773
You let a piece of land,

587
00:24:22.695 --> 00:24:24.598
give it some water, vegetation grows.

588
00:24:24.598 --> 00:24:29.217
You leave a piece of land, opportunity for human mind,

589
00:24:29.217 --> 00:24:31.664
and everything grows.

590
00:24:31.664 --> 00:24:35.777
So I say that, life is about management of expectations.

591
00:24:35.777 --> 00:24:37.287
And the worst thing we could have done,

592
00:24:37.287 --> 00:24:39.157
and this is gonna be controversial,

593
00:24:39.157 --> 00:24:42.242
is to create expectation in women.

594
00:24:42.242 --> 00:24:44.477
Because what we lost in these 20,

595
00:24:44.477 --> 00:24:46.389
or what we made in these 20 years,

596
00:24:46.389 --> 00:24:48.389
was the most important thing as human beings

597
00:24:48.389 --> 00:24:52.257
that we can create, which is opportunity for hope,

598
00:24:52.257 --> 00:24:56.134
opportunity for the brain to manifest its full potential.

599
00:24:56.134 --> 00:24:58.210
And we saw that incredibly,

600
00:24:58.210 --> 00:25:00.648
and Ayesha saw that in women that she dealt with.

601
00:25:00.648 --> 00:25:01.606
Right.

602
00:25:01.606 --> 00:25:05.773
I mean, I go in and I thought that I'm talented.

603
00:25:07.331 --> 00:25:11.695
When you bring hungry people, and give them opportunity,

604
00:25:11.695 --> 00:25:14.393
they would blow me away, as far as their talent.

605
00:25:14.393 --> 00:25:16.149
They would learn multiple languages.

606
00:25:16.149 --> 00:25:20.544
One person actually got two degrees within two years.

607
00:25:20.544 --> 00:25:21.559
The women were learning...

608
00:25:21.559 --> 00:25:22.887
I mean, you can speak to that.

609
00:25:22.887 --> 00:25:23.720
Oh my gosh! Yeah.

610
00:25:23.720 --> 00:25:28.534
The level of motivation, that hunger for growth,

611
00:25:28.534 --> 00:25:30.451
for knowledge, for art,

612
00:25:31.543 --> 00:25:35.359
I've never seen that in my life anywhere.

613
00:25:35.359 --> 00:25:37.799
And I think it's because of that contrast,

614
00:25:37.799 --> 00:25:39.387
where there was nothing,

615
00:25:39.387 --> 00:25:41.292
and then suddenly they were given opportunity

616
00:25:41.292 --> 00:25:43.536
that they could do anything they wanted.

617
00:25:43.536 --> 00:25:47.202
And they all chose to be something significant,

618
00:25:47.202 --> 00:25:50.304
not just in their own lives, because they you realized that

619
00:25:50.304 --> 00:25:54.825
in a country where you usually have a group of survivors,

620
00:25:54.825 --> 00:25:56.862
Afghans are mostly survivors.

621
00:25:56.862 --> 00:25:58.974
They've seen governments come and go.

622
00:25:58.974 --> 00:26:03.266
They've seen countries come and go, empires come and go.

623
00:26:03.266 --> 00:26:05.878
And so they have this sense of

624
00:26:05.878 --> 00:26:08.853
self-worth and self-preservation.

625
00:26:08.853 --> 00:26:10.676
But this new generation of women

626
00:26:10.676 --> 00:26:13.397
knew that it has to be beyond that.

627
00:26:13.397 --> 00:26:15.343
It can't just be about the self,

628
00:26:15.343 --> 00:26:20.140
it has to be recreation of stable grounds on which they,

629
00:26:20.140 --> 00:26:22.902
their children and the next generation can live.

630
00:26:22.902 --> 00:26:24.570
So over the past 20 years,

631
00:26:24.570 --> 00:26:27.779
what we've seen is these incredible women

632
00:26:27.779 --> 00:26:30.377
create the infrastructure for themselves

633
00:26:30.377 --> 00:26:33.713
and for the next generation to grow,

634
00:26:33.713 --> 00:26:37.140
and to be someone significant, and to have a voice,

635
00:26:37.140 --> 00:26:39.784
and to propel that voice to the world.

636
00:26:39.784 --> 00:26:41.737
Which makes the removal of that hope

637
00:26:41.737 --> 00:26:43.038
and all of those opportunities,

638
00:26:43.038 --> 00:26:44.509
all the more tragic, of course.

639
00:26:44.509 --> 00:26:45.342
Devastating.

640
00:26:45.342 --> 00:26:47.980
And I think we can debate the pros and cons

641
00:26:47.980 --> 00:26:52.480
of the US withdrawal, and this sense of the occupation

642
00:26:54.529 --> 00:26:58.199
over the last 20 years being an unmitigated boondoggle,

643
00:26:58.199 --> 00:27:00.601
and political disaster, et cetera.

644
00:27:00.601 --> 00:27:02.354
Certainly we can agree that

645
00:27:02.354 --> 00:27:06.271
the withdrawal was bungled on a colossal scale.

646
00:27:07.965 --> 00:27:09.798
But help us understand

647
00:27:11.298 --> 00:27:15.465
the human rights implications of where we are now.

648
00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:20.257
I think it was just one

649
00:27:20.257 --> 00:27:23.424
of the most poorly planned steps ever.

650
00:27:25.673 --> 00:27:27.895
And we could go into the details of that,

651
00:27:27.895 --> 00:27:29.272
but I think in many ways,

652
00:27:29.272 --> 00:27:32.603
it's a disrespect to everyone who's worked there.

653
00:27:32.603 --> 00:27:34.306
It's a disrespect for all the troops

654
00:27:34.306 --> 00:27:37.033
who actually lost their lives there.

655
00:27:37.033 --> 00:27:39.400
I look at it from a very sexist way.

656
00:27:39.400 --> 00:27:42.466
I think it was a very misogynistic step.

657
00:27:42.466 --> 00:27:45.442
I mean, yes, there has been a lot of focus on

658
00:27:45.442 --> 00:27:47.104
translators and allies

659
00:27:47.104 --> 00:27:51.497
who helped the troops do their job and protect them.

660
00:27:51.497 --> 00:27:53.167
But did anybody think about

661
00:27:53.167 --> 00:27:55.892
what was going to happen to the women?

662
00:27:55.892 --> 00:28:00.442
Did anybody ever consider the girls in Afghanistan?

663
00:28:00.442 --> 00:28:02.709
That conversation never comes up.

664
00:28:02.709 --> 00:28:07.064
Did they never see that if Taliban come back,

665
00:28:07.064 --> 00:28:08.689
that they're basically going to ask them

666
00:28:08.689 --> 00:28:10.439
to not exist anymore?

667
00:28:11.856 --> 00:28:13.771
That's exactly what's happening.

668
00:28:13.771 --> 00:28:16.535
Nobody ever thought about women.

669
00:28:16.535 --> 00:28:18.088
They were never part of the plan.

670
00:28:18.088 --> 00:28:19.033
Yeah.

671
00:28:19.033 --> 00:28:20.422
It really wasn't.

672
00:28:20.422 --> 00:28:22.170
And what's interesting about this,

673
00:28:22.170 --> 00:28:26.392
in the context of the advisability of US withdrawing or not,

674
00:28:26.392 --> 00:28:31.033
is the downstream implications of that withdrawal,

675
00:28:31.033 --> 00:28:33.783
and this idea that the occupation

676
00:28:34.896 --> 00:28:36.442
was a bad idea to begin with,

677
00:28:36.442 --> 00:28:39.414
and it was a loser, and it was never gonna...

678
00:28:39.414 --> 00:28:40.917
We were never gonna nation build

679
00:28:40.917 --> 00:28:43.207
to the extent that we aspire to.

680
00:28:43.207 --> 00:28:46.794
And yet there were victories along the way,

681
00:28:46.794 --> 00:28:48.175
and the hope and the opportunities

682
00:28:48.175 --> 00:28:49.845
that were created for these women,

683
00:28:49.845 --> 00:28:51.108
and a big part of your message

684
00:28:51.108 --> 00:28:54.359
is recognizing that some good was done,

685
00:28:54.359 --> 00:28:57.375
which begs the question of whether or not

686
00:28:57.375 --> 00:29:00.125
some form of sustained occupation

687
00:29:01.405 --> 00:29:04.786
could have been advisable in this situation,

688
00:29:04.786 --> 00:29:07.508
to maintain some level of stability

689
00:29:07.508 --> 00:29:09.824
for this type of opportunity.

690
00:29:09.824 --> 00:29:11.425
Absolutely.

691
00:29:11.425 --> 00:29:13.831
I would say that I agree with you,

692
00:29:13.831 --> 00:29:17.498
protracted military existence in Afghanistan

693
00:29:18.357 --> 00:29:19.542
was not a tenable thing.

694
00:29:19.542 --> 00:29:22.352
Not at the numbers that people spoke about.

695
00:29:22.352 --> 00:29:24.945
The small numbers may be, and we have how many...?

696
00:29:24.945 --> 00:29:27.929
We are in over 100 countries right now, just maintaining.

697
00:29:27.929 --> 00:29:28.762
And we were doing well.

698
00:29:28.762 --> 00:29:30.098
How long have we been in South Korea?

699
00:29:30.098 --> 00:29:30.931
Oh my gosh.

700
00:29:30.931 --> 00:29:31.892
Yeah, 50 years or more.

701
00:29:31.892 --> 00:29:33.094
Exactly.

702
00:29:33.094 --> 00:29:36.186
So the reason Ayesha brings up

703
00:29:36.186 --> 00:29:38.747
the misogynistic and sexist terms,

704
00:29:38.747 --> 00:29:42.164
is because insouciance has a connotation.

705
00:29:43.144 --> 00:29:45.302
Why would you be indifferent or unaware

706
00:29:45.302 --> 00:29:50.302
or willfully unaware of what has been done in Afghanistan?

707
00:29:50.549 --> 00:29:52.634
Which is not a small matter.

708
00:29:52.634 --> 00:29:53.696
Forget about nation building.

709
00:29:53.696 --> 00:29:56.633
You don't go to nations to build buildings,

710
00:29:56.633 --> 00:29:59.636
you go to nations to build human opportunities.

711
00:29:59.636 --> 00:30:02.237
If that's even an option, but even if it's not,

712
00:30:02.237 --> 00:30:05.001
if it's a by-product, you acknowledge it.

713
00:30:05.001 --> 00:30:08.614
Why is it that building of 16 million women,

714
00:30:08.614 --> 00:30:12.275
to the degree where each of them were incredibly talented,

715
00:30:12.275 --> 00:30:14.521
is not even being acknowledged as a victory.

716
00:30:14.521 --> 00:30:16.718
If nothing else, we did a good job.

717
00:30:16.718 --> 00:30:19.156
The willful insouciance,

718
00:30:19.156 --> 00:30:21.906
the willful ignoring of that fact

719
00:30:23.504 --> 00:30:25.869
says something about us ignoring it.

720
00:30:25.869 --> 00:30:29.939
And then the fact that leaving that vacuum in place,

721
00:30:29.939 --> 00:30:33.333
forget about the vacuum, it's gonna be shrouded, literally.

722
00:30:33.333 --> 00:30:36.227
We talk about Taliban making women wear the burqas,

723
00:30:36.227 --> 00:30:37.650
which covers completely.

724
00:30:37.650 --> 00:30:39.322
Well, it's literally unfair

725
00:30:39.322 --> 00:30:42.906
to lead that across 16 million people,

726
00:30:42.906 --> 00:30:46.215
where that's the death of 16 million people.

727
00:30:46.215 --> 00:30:48.173
Why is that not being talked about?

728
00:30:48.173 --> 00:30:50.295
I don't wanna make this a political thing,

729
00:30:50.295 --> 00:30:54.011
or sexist male, but you have to go to that core.

730
00:30:54.011 --> 00:30:56.574
Why isn't that being addressed?

731
00:30:56.574 --> 00:30:58.329
And that's an important question to ask,

732
00:30:58.329 --> 00:31:01.505
because that question comes to us here in the United States,

733
00:31:01.505 --> 00:31:03.539
that question will come to us in Europe,

734
00:31:03.539 --> 00:31:06.062
that question will come to us everywhere in the world.

735
00:31:06.062 --> 00:31:10.851
If we don't acknowledge the value in the language we use,

736
00:31:10.851 --> 00:31:15.184
we'll live the consequences in the policies we live.

737
00:31:16.928 --> 00:31:21.511
It's funny 'cause when you sent your message to Rich,

738
00:31:22.495 --> 00:31:24.530
that email, then he shared it with me,

739
00:31:24.530 --> 00:31:26.197
when you guys were getting in contact

740
00:31:26.197 --> 00:31:28.888
about coming back on to discuss these issues,

741
00:31:28.888 --> 00:31:31.808
the idea of reframing the 20 years as a triumph,

742
00:31:31.808 --> 00:31:34.095
when everyone was talking about, I think personally,

743
00:31:34.095 --> 00:31:35.412
I think because of the swiftness

744
00:31:35.412 --> 00:31:38.041
with which the Taliban took over,

745
00:31:38.041 --> 00:31:40.285
that that dialogue of failure

746
00:31:40.285 --> 00:31:42.530
has been the main talking point,

747
00:31:42.530 --> 00:31:44.565
I think that has something to do with that.

748
00:31:44.565 --> 00:31:46.720
But the idea of reframing it

749
00:31:46.720 --> 00:31:50.091
as one of the best things the United States taxpayers

750
00:31:50.091 --> 00:31:51.968
have ever done is very interesting.

751
00:31:51.968 --> 00:31:52.982
It's definitely something

752
00:31:52.982 --> 00:31:55.263
I haven't heard anywhere else besides that email,

753
00:31:55.263 --> 00:31:56.652
and now what you're saying today.

754
00:31:56.652 --> 00:31:58.981
And I think that's a really powerful point,

755
00:31:58.981 --> 00:32:03.124
that there was some costs of doing business element,

756
00:32:03.124 --> 00:32:04.466
there was a corruption element,

757
00:32:04.466 --> 00:32:06.710
some money went away that shouldn't have gone,

758
00:32:06.710 --> 00:32:08.259
but maybe that is the cost of doing business

759
00:32:08.259 --> 00:32:12.212
of reclaiming lives that had been erased.

760
00:32:12.212 --> 00:32:15.069
And maybe it is the world's responsibility,

761
00:32:15.069 --> 00:32:17.308
considering Afghanistan is this country

762
00:32:17.308 --> 00:32:20.033
that since the silk road, has dealt with...

763
00:32:20.033 --> 00:32:24.748
Is like has been part of globalization since then, right?

764
00:32:24.748 --> 00:32:27.848
I mean, it's hung in the balance between

765
00:32:27.848 --> 00:32:31.305
whether it's the Soviet and the US in the seventies,

766
00:32:31.305 --> 00:32:35.310
or the British, when they were in Burma in India,

767
00:32:35.310 --> 00:32:38.071
and here's Afghanistan, and trying to figure it out,

768
00:32:38.071 --> 00:32:40.312
and even well before, Alexander The Great,

769
00:32:40.312 --> 00:32:41.951
and the Persian empires.

770
00:32:41.951 --> 00:32:46.034
Afghanistan has been this one place that has been

771
00:32:47.165 --> 00:32:51.248
at the core of so much foreign policy for centuries.

772
00:32:51.248 --> 00:32:52.505
I wonder if you could speak to that,

773
00:32:52.505 --> 00:32:56.922
given your knowledge of the history there, and how...

774
00:32:58.006 --> 00:33:01.264
Take us from that to where we are now, historically,

775
00:33:01.264 --> 00:33:04.431
and why Afghanistan has been so vital.

776
00:33:06.168 --> 00:33:08.405
We're not much of nationalists,

777
00:33:08.405 --> 00:33:11.708
so the borders are not as important as the people.

778
00:33:11.708 --> 00:33:15.549
But in this case, the borders, although they have moved,

779
00:33:15.549 --> 00:33:18.156
it's a unique little real estate.

780
00:33:18.156 --> 00:33:20.812
I wouldn't wanna own land in that real estate,

781
00:33:20.812 --> 00:33:25.479
where your neighbors are, Russia, China, Pakistan, Iran,

782
00:33:26.659 --> 00:33:28.397
and a little further out India.

783
00:33:28.397 --> 00:33:29.389
That's a bad neighborhood.

784
00:33:29.389 --> 00:33:31.388
And you're landlocked.

785
00:33:31.388 --> 00:33:33.701
And not only are you landlocked,

786
00:33:33.701 --> 00:33:36.218
the airplanes where they fly out of Kabul,

787
00:33:36.218 --> 00:33:38.823
they have to use special flight maneuvers,

788
00:33:38.823 --> 00:33:42.113
because it's a bowl with tall mountains.

789
00:33:42.113 --> 00:33:43.287
And everywhere it's like that.

790
00:33:43.287 --> 00:33:46.210
So it's just mountainous land,

791
00:33:46.210 --> 00:33:47.915
beautiful though, absolutely gorgeous.

792
00:33:47.915 --> 00:33:50.590
I remember the first time I went there in 2002,

793
00:33:50.590 --> 00:33:54.537
they took me in a Russian helicopter, and by the way,

794
00:33:54.537 --> 00:33:56.955
I never wanna do that again, because it was an old...

795
00:33:56.955 --> 00:33:58.177
You saw the wires coming out.

796
00:33:58.177 --> 00:34:00.007
They said, "Don't worry, it's gonna be very safe.

797
00:34:00.007 --> 00:34:02.359
Not many people have died from this flight."

798
00:34:02.359 --> 00:34:04.995
And we fly over the mountains, right just barely...

799
00:34:04.995 --> 00:34:06.499
This has Hindu Kush mountains,

800
00:34:06.499 --> 00:34:07.474
which are some of the tallest,

801
00:34:07.474 --> 00:34:09.429
I think the second tallest in the world.

802
00:34:09.429 --> 00:34:12.429
And then we get to this place called Badakhshan,

803
00:34:12.429 --> 00:34:16.330
which is green valleys, little river rats running,

804
00:34:16.330 --> 00:34:17.549
and people are running around.

805
00:34:17.549 --> 00:34:18.799
And I'm like...

806
00:34:19.787 --> 00:34:22.761
I'm crying like a baby, like, "This beauty!"

807
00:34:22.761 --> 00:34:24.755
So it's an incredible land

808
00:34:24.755 --> 00:34:27.556
as far as its extremes, as far as...

809
00:34:27.556 --> 00:34:29.907
And the people are a group of different kinds of peoples

810
00:34:29.907 --> 00:34:34.074
from youngest conquests, to Alexander's conquests.

811
00:34:36.236 --> 00:34:39.198
You see remnants of those features in Afghanistan as well,

812
00:34:39.198 --> 00:34:41.972
as well as some others as well.

813
00:34:41.972 --> 00:34:45.917
So it's a unique population that has been forged in

814
00:34:45.917 --> 00:34:50.500
the crucible of the most difficult space in the planet.

815
00:34:51.425 --> 00:34:55.508
And that's because of the China, Russia, India?

816
00:34:55.508 --> 00:34:56.899
Is that the reason this-?

817
00:34:56.899 --> 00:34:58.086
Pakistan.

818
00:34:58.086 --> 00:35:01.856
The Pakistan, and on top of that, the environment itself,

819
00:35:01.856 --> 00:35:03.727
the mountainous environment...

820
00:35:03.727 --> 00:35:05.847
Like for example, the Panjshir valley just fell,

821
00:35:05.847 --> 00:35:06.984
which is this little space

822
00:35:06.984 --> 00:35:08.332
that had not fallen to the Taliban.

823
00:35:08.332 --> 00:35:11.375
And the reason for that is because external forces, again.

824
00:35:11.375 --> 00:35:13.536
The Russians bombed that place,

825
00:35:13.536 --> 00:35:16.138
Khabarov bombed that place nine times over,

826
00:35:16.138 --> 00:35:18.378
still they couldn't conquer it.

827
00:35:18.378 --> 00:35:20.110
They couldn't conquer the place.

828
00:35:20.110 --> 00:35:23.479
So the geography is just crazy.

829
00:35:23.479 --> 00:35:25.604
When you go there, it's just absolutely...

830
00:35:25.604 --> 00:35:27.754
For nine miles, it takes you four hours

831
00:35:27.754 --> 00:35:30.473
because you have to go these incredible traverse.

832
00:35:30.473 --> 00:35:32.967
So the environment, the landscape,

833
00:35:32.967 --> 00:35:36.980
the neighbors has created these people that are survivors,

834
00:35:36.980 --> 00:35:41.313
caring, loving people that are unique to the region.

835
00:35:42.380 --> 00:35:47.380
But at the same time, it's constant war, constant calamity.

836
00:35:47.555 --> 00:35:48.718
I said, "Please..."

837
00:35:48.718 --> 00:35:50.237
And this again, you've gotta be politically,

838
00:35:50.237 --> 00:35:51.421
I'm known for this.

839
00:35:51.421 --> 00:35:55.361
"Sell the land, buy some space in Florida."

840
00:35:55.361 --> 00:35:57.948
Because it's a tough, tough neighborhood.

841
00:35:57.948 --> 00:35:59.527
It's a tough neighborhood.

842
00:35:59.527 --> 00:36:03.195
But because of that then, the women up to now,

843
00:36:03.195 --> 00:36:05.145
even under Taliban, yeah they shroud them,

844
00:36:05.145 --> 00:36:06.476
very tough women.

845
00:36:06.476 --> 00:36:10.123
These are really resilient people.

846
00:36:10.123 --> 00:36:12.077
I think the withdrawal,

847
00:36:12.077 --> 00:36:16.160
the timing of the withdrawal speaks to the uptick

848
00:36:17.557 --> 00:36:21.213
that we're seeing in Neo isolationism across the world.

849
00:36:21.213 --> 00:36:24.566
So it's not that surprising that it occurred now,

850
00:36:24.566 --> 00:36:27.386
but I think the manner in which we conducted it,

851
00:36:27.386 --> 00:36:29.345
and the mistakes that were made,

852
00:36:29.345 --> 00:36:33.819
and the kind of implications that we're now living with,

853
00:36:33.819 --> 00:36:38.819
really underscores the decline of the United States

854
00:36:38.865 --> 00:36:42.197
as a responsible superpower in so many ways.

855
00:36:42.197 --> 00:36:46.096
I mean, how can any allies trust us again?

856
00:36:46.096 --> 00:36:50.243
How can our friends across the world believe us

857
00:36:50.243 --> 00:36:52.704
when we say we're coming here to do X, Y, and Z,

858
00:36:52.704 --> 00:36:55.545
when we pull out and create the kind of chaos and havoc

859
00:36:55.545 --> 00:36:57.337
that we're now seeing.

860
00:36:57.337 --> 00:37:00.298
And from a boots on the ground perspective,

861
00:37:00.298 --> 00:37:04.631
I wanna know a little bit more about your experience

862
00:37:06.556 --> 00:37:08.630
working there with women,

863
00:37:08.630 --> 00:37:10.827
specifically in these initiatives that you created,

864
00:37:10.827 --> 00:37:15.660
and the legacy of those today, and what's going to happen.

865
00:37:17.645 --> 00:37:20.108
I actually want to highlight

866
00:37:20.108 --> 00:37:22.760
what Adam was referring to earlier.

867
00:37:22.760 --> 00:37:25.793
This is exactly what we saw when we went back,

868
00:37:25.793 --> 00:37:29.992
is a lot of our friends here in the United States

869
00:37:29.992 --> 00:37:33.595
and around the world always refer to

870
00:37:33.595 --> 00:37:36.542
US's existence in Afghanistan as a big failure,

871
00:37:36.542 --> 00:37:40.792
but the positivity and the success that was created

872
00:37:42.921 --> 00:37:46.035
during these last 20 years was probably...

873
00:37:46.035 --> 00:37:49.470
It's something that we all need to talk about and highlight.

874
00:37:49.470 --> 00:37:50.723
And I think it's something

875
00:37:50.723 --> 00:37:54.378
that we all need to be very proud of.

876
00:37:54.378 --> 00:37:58.628
You have a generation of women scientists, doctors,

877
00:38:00.061 --> 00:38:04.061
lawyers, engineers, business women, politicians.

878
00:38:06.833 --> 00:38:08.655
We even have the robotics team,

879
00:38:08.655 --> 00:38:11.238
just incredible human resource.

880
00:38:13.654 --> 00:38:18.001
This is talent that was allowed to flourish.

881
00:38:18.001 --> 00:38:21.807
And these people, not only represented just Afghanistan,

882
00:38:21.807 --> 00:38:24.175
but they represented the best part

883
00:38:24.175 --> 00:38:26.919
of who we are as a human race.

884
00:38:26.919 --> 00:38:30.823
And working with women in Afghanistan,

885
00:38:30.823 --> 00:38:33.323
and seeing them take advantage

886
00:38:34.214 --> 00:38:36.797
of the security was incredible.

887
00:38:37.739 --> 00:38:40.664
And you always compare yourself.

888
00:38:40.664 --> 00:38:41.695
I always compared myself

889
00:38:41.695 --> 00:38:43.496
to some of the women who would work there,

890
00:38:43.496 --> 00:38:47.163
and with the speed of acclimating to change,

891
00:38:48.221 --> 00:38:52.459
and the speed or the hunger that they had

892
00:38:52.459 --> 00:38:55.590
of getting themselves trained in a particular field,

893
00:38:55.590 --> 00:38:57.990
is something I've never seen ever in my life.

894
00:38:57.990 --> 00:39:02.866
And it really shifted the narrative in the society.

895
00:39:02.866 --> 00:39:03.878
And I think one of the reasons

896
00:39:03.878 --> 00:39:06.409
why we saw this massive growth,

897
00:39:06.409 --> 00:39:08.678
where women became leaders,

898
00:39:08.678 --> 00:39:11.172
and you saw change in the society,

899
00:39:11.172 --> 00:39:16.010
was because of the presence of US, and the west there,

900
00:39:16.010 --> 00:39:18.766
that allowed them to flourish and thrive.

901
00:39:18.766 --> 00:39:20.792
And Dean and I were involved

902
00:39:20.792 --> 00:39:23.548
in creating health empowerment programs,

903
00:39:23.548 --> 00:39:25.967
and Dean actually did an amazing job

904
00:39:25.967 --> 00:39:28.592
in the ministry of health, where he created

905
00:39:28.592 --> 00:39:31.030
the most successful health empowerment program,

906
00:39:31.030 --> 00:39:32.842
without ever challenging

907
00:39:32.842 --> 00:39:37.274
the traditions and the cultural norms and the impediments

908
00:39:37.274 --> 00:39:38.249
that were there.

909
00:39:38.249 --> 00:39:39.111
Yeah.

910
00:39:39.111 --> 00:39:41.437
I called it social jujitsu.

911
00:39:41.437 --> 00:39:44.951
I'm terrible with jujitsu, but that's (indistinct).

912
00:39:44.951 --> 00:39:46.823
You're good at social jujitsu.

913
00:39:46.823 --> 00:39:49.323
Which is, you don't confront

914
00:39:50.423 --> 00:39:54.071
longstanding traditions and edicts, right?

915
00:39:54.071 --> 00:39:55.361
It's impossible to challenge.

916
00:39:55.361 --> 00:39:57.069
So you create a pathway there

917
00:39:57.069 --> 00:39:58.696
for the behavior to come forward.

918
00:39:58.696 --> 00:40:02.662
And human behavior will find its path of least resistance,

919
00:40:02.662 --> 00:40:03.678
it will.

920
00:40:03.678 --> 00:40:04.511
So one of the projects...

921
00:40:04.511 --> 00:40:05.966
And by the way, I've said this in the past,

922
00:40:05.966 --> 00:40:08.774
I'm gonna retract that, which is, I created that.

923
00:40:08.774 --> 00:40:09.607
I did not create that.

924
00:40:09.607 --> 00:40:11.761
I led that, or I led it from behind,

925
00:40:11.761 --> 00:40:13.415
because these people did it themselves.

926
00:40:13.415 --> 00:40:15.505
I wanna make sure that we don't...

927
00:40:15.505 --> 00:40:18.680
This leadership thing is overstated.

928
00:40:18.680 --> 00:40:20.934
The environment was created, I kind of created the idea,

929
00:40:20.934 --> 00:40:22.511
but you should see what happens.

930
00:40:22.511 --> 00:40:25.668
So one of that projects was, where we said,

931
00:40:25.668 --> 00:40:26.794
"We're gonna go to these places,

932
00:40:26.794 --> 00:40:28.921
which were Taliban infested."

933
00:40:28.921 --> 00:40:30.164
And they still need healthcare,

934
00:40:30.164 --> 00:40:34.081
because at the time in 2002 average age was 48.

935
00:40:35.666 --> 00:40:38.902
One out of four children were dying before the age of five,

936
00:40:38.902 --> 00:40:42.851
one out of six women were dying during their pregnancy life.

937
00:40:42.851 --> 00:40:44.478
Given that the average pregnant woman

938
00:40:44.478 --> 00:40:46.605
had seven pregnancies, you do the math.

939
00:40:46.605 --> 00:40:48.313
And they didn't just die,

940
00:40:48.313 --> 00:40:49.606
it's the worst death you can imagine,

941
00:40:49.606 --> 00:40:51.356
because they started bleeding,

942
00:40:51.356 --> 00:40:54.632
there's no hospital within four horseback rides.

943
00:40:54.632 --> 00:40:57.662
So they're going to die of severe bleeding and pain,

944
00:40:57.662 --> 00:40:59.741
getting to the hospital by the thousands.

945
00:40:59.741 --> 00:41:01.823
So that was my challenge, coming from NIH,

946
00:41:01.823 --> 00:41:04.344
the richest research institute in the world.

947
00:41:04.344 --> 00:41:07.303
And where for a project for tapping,

948
00:41:07.303 --> 00:41:09.798
we spent like $6 million.

949
00:41:09.798 --> 00:41:12.278
Where here you didn't have penicillin.

950
00:41:12.278 --> 00:41:13.888
So what we did was,

951
00:41:13.888 --> 00:41:16.219
we knew that the lowest denominator of healthcare,

952
00:41:16.219 --> 00:41:18.512
which would save 90% was ORS,

953
00:41:18.512 --> 00:41:22.869
which is Oral Rehydration Fluid, water, salt, and sugar,

954
00:41:22.869 --> 00:41:26.644
because the children that died from diarrhea, dysentery,

955
00:41:26.644 --> 00:41:28.235
and you can't give medicine for that,

956
00:41:28.235 --> 00:41:30.948
you just make sure that the water is retained.

957
00:41:30.948 --> 00:41:32.438
So because of no clean water?

958
00:41:32.438 --> 00:41:33.271
No clean water.

959
00:41:33.271 --> 00:41:34.482
It's like a homemade Gatorade.

960
00:41:34.482 --> 00:41:35.791
Home made Gatorade, yeah.

961
00:41:35.791 --> 00:41:38.866
Yeah , so it's about water sources

962
00:41:38.866 --> 00:41:41.549
and that creates the dysentery that you can't recover from

963
00:41:41.549 --> 00:41:43.506
if you're under five.
Exactly. Exactly.

964
00:41:43.506 --> 00:41:46.330
Women died on the other ones upper respiratory,

965
00:41:46.330 --> 00:41:47.874
one antibiotic ampicillin

966
00:41:47.874 --> 00:41:49.616
would save significant amount of people.

967
00:41:49.616 --> 00:41:53.216
And then women knowing that when they're bleeding

968
00:41:53.216 --> 00:41:55.604
this is spotting as opposed to serious bleeding,

969
00:41:55.604 --> 00:41:56.719
so start now.

970
00:41:56.719 --> 00:41:58.234
And that save thousands.

971
00:41:58.234 --> 00:41:59.523
So who do you need for that?

972
00:41:59.523 --> 00:42:01.150
Do you need doctors? No.

973
00:42:01.150 --> 00:42:02.119
It's useless in Afghanistan.

974
00:42:02.119 --> 00:42:03.301
Do you need hospitals? No.

975
00:42:03.301 --> 00:42:05.289
Although all the people who had money,

976
00:42:05.289 --> 00:42:07.192
wanted to build hospitals, why?

977
00:42:07.192 --> 00:42:08.101
You could show it on TV.

978
00:42:08.101 --> 00:42:09.658
It's all about visibility.
Visibility.

979
00:42:09.658 --> 00:42:10.491
Now, I said,

980
00:42:10.491 --> 00:42:11.610
"Spend it on these young girls,

981
00:42:11.610 --> 00:42:13.941
sixth grade educated young ladies,

982
00:42:13.941 --> 00:42:16.895
who would learn the basics."

983
00:42:16.895 --> 00:42:19.296
So we went to the regions, Taliban leaders,

984
00:42:19.296 --> 00:42:21.782
and we say, "We want to bring healthcare."

985
00:42:21.782 --> 00:42:22.995
They said, "No, no, we don't need it.

986
00:42:22.995 --> 00:42:23.828
We don't need your doctors."

987
00:42:23.828 --> 00:42:26.662
"No, no, no, no. Your own girls."

988
00:42:26.662 --> 00:42:28.747
And they would only see women and children.

989
00:42:28.747 --> 00:42:30.121
"Is that really?

990
00:42:30.121 --> 00:42:31.379
Okay. Okay. Sure."

991
00:42:31.379 --> 00:42:33.505
But I knew what was gonna happen.

992
00:42:33.505 --> 00:42:35.246
We made sure for most part,

993
00:42:35.246 --> 00:42:37.241
the clinics were made in a conspicuous place,

994
00:42:37.241 --> 00:42:39.109
so everybody could see,

995
00:42:39.109 --> 00:42:41.228
because we knew what happens in three months,

996
00:42:41.228 --> 00:42:42.973
guess who gets sick?

997
00:42:42.973 --> 00:42:44.888
The man, the big man.

998
00:42:44.888 --> 00:42:47.934
And he would go to this young girl in a prostrate position,

999
00:42:47.934 --> 00:42:49.352
saying, "Can you help me?"

1000
00:42:49.352 --> 00:42:51.604
You just flipped the dynamics.

1001
00:42:51.604 --> 00:42:54.882
That woman was now empowered, that woman became a doctor.

1002
00:42:54.882 --> 00:42:57.294
Not really, by concept.

1003
00:42:57.294 --> 00:42:59.776
And a lot of women ended up in the parliament,

1004
00:42:59.776 --> 00:43:03.163
a lot of those women ended up in positions of power.

1005
00:43:03.163 --> 00:43:05.361
So you're training medics?
How do you do that.

1006
00:43:05.361 --> 00:43:06.246
(crosstalk drowns out the speaker)

1007
00:43:06.246 --> 00:43:07.971
You giving them basically EMT training.

1008
00:43:07.971 --> 00:43:09.407
Less than EMT.

1009
00:43:09.407 --> 00:43:14.407
On the kind of jujitsu tip, how do you accomplish that,

1010
00:43:14.662 --> 00:43:17.080
in a Taliban controlled area?

1011
00:43:17.080 --> 00:43:19.362
Because any female empowerment,

1012
00:43:19.362 --> 00:43:23.179
is a visible threat to the power hierarchy.

1013
00:43:23.179 --> 00:43:24.554
Education is a threat,

1014
00:43:24.554 --> 00:43:26.432
but healthcare for the women themselves,

1015
00:43:26.432 --> 00:43:29.635
their wives, and their daughters, and children is not.

1016
00:43:29.635 --> 00:43:31.503
For them to serve each other.

1017
00:43:31.503 --> 00:43:32.336
Correct.
Right.

1018
00:43:32.336 --> 00:43:33.169
Correct.

1019
00:43:33.169 --> 00:43:35.290
So it was just, "Oh women can go play around it,

1020
00:43:35.290 --> 00:43:36.533
and take care of each other."

1021
00:43:36.533 --> 00:43:38.868
Well, we knew healthcare is healthcare.

1022
00:43:38.868 --> 00:43:41.072
So we actually wrote a paper,

1023
00:43:41.072 --> 00:43:43.024
published in peer review journal.

1024
00:43:43.024 --> 00:43:45.515
How to empower women in third world countries.

1025
00:43:45.515 --> 00:43:47.214
The title was Recalcitrant Countries,

1026
00:43:47.214 --> 00:43:48.874
but they changed it, so third world.

1027
00:43:48.874 --> 00:43:52.269
So nonetheless that's done, but that wasn't us.

1028
00:43:52.269 --> 00:43:53.994
So healthcare just being the vehicle

1029
00:43:53.994 --> 00:43:55.701
for empowerment, right?

1030
00:43:55.701 --> 00:43:56.534
Yeah.

1031
00:43:56.534 --> 00:43:58.795
Because it's a non-threatening, education is threatening.

1032
00:43:58.795 --> 00:44:02.771
Ampicillin is not, you're just giving it.

1033
00:44:02.771 --> 00:44:05.019
But the bigger picture here was that,

1034
00:44:05.019 --> 00:44:07.375
these women took that position,

1035
00:44:07.375 --> 00:44:09.495
and took it to the nth power.

1036
00:44:09.495 --> 00:44:12.670
Because they became leaders, they became outspoken leaders,

1037
00:44:12.670 --> 00:44:15.670
they did more than just healthcare.

1038
00:44:15.670 --> 00:44:17.489
And you just created the environment.

1039
00:44:17.489 --> 00:44:20.676
And if that's not success, if that's not success,

1040
00:44:20.676 --> 00:44:22.477
so either we consider success that,

1041
00:44:22.477 --> 00:44:24.592
how much wealth we draw from a country,

1042
00:44:24.592 --> 00:44:27.654
or how much wealth human value,

1043
00:44:27.654 --> 00:44:30.015
human capital, we leave behind.

1044
00:44:30.015 --> 00:44:33.498
The human capital per capita if that's a quantifiable thing,

1045
00:44:33.498 --> 00:44:36.081
was exponential in Afghanistan.

1046
00:44:36.959 --> 00:44:40.116
From what the base was, to what we left it.

1047
00:44:40.116 --> 00:44:42.798
And it's gonna be exponential the other way around now.

1048
00:44:42.798 --> 00:44:43.712
Yeah.

